# Security & Access Control

## Sign-in (Feature 1, Feature 3)
1.  `POST /login` — verify the Google ID token (`googleCredentials`); read `email`, `given_name`, `family_name`. Normalize email with `trim().toLowerCase()`; only `oc.edu` and its subdomains are accepted.
2.  A new email creates the user — the **only** way users are created (FR-013). There is no registration endpoint and there are no passwords.
3.  Reuse an unexpired Session row for that user; otherwise sign a JWT (24h) and create a Session row.
4.  Respond with `{ userId, email, firstName, lastName, roles, token }` (roles from `toRolesResponse`).
5.  `POST /logout` — delete the Session row for the Bearer token.
6.  `POST /dev-login` (FR-014) — development only: `404` unless `DEV_LOGIN=true` **and** `NODE_ENV !== "production"`; signs in an existing user with the shared `DEV_LOGIN_PASSWORD`. Never add another password or bypass path.
7.  Secrets (`AUTH_SECRET`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`) come from the environment; never hardcode them.

## Request Authentication
*   `authenticate` reads `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, requires an unexpired Session row, and loads the user **with roles from the database on every request** — a role change applies on the user's next request.
*   It sets `req.user = { id, roles: [{ id, role, areaId }] }`. Never trust ids or roles sent in the request body.
*   Missing/invalid token → `401` `{ message: "Unauthorized! ..." }`.

## Role Guards
Roles: `admin`, `area_admin` (one row per area, with `areaId`), `maintenance`. Every user is also a requester without a row. All guards live in `app/authorization/authorization.js`:

| Export | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `authenticate` | Any signed-in user |
| `authenticateAdmin` | `admin` only |
| `authenticateMaintenance` | `maintenance` (or admin) |
| `authenticateAreaAdmin` | `area_admin` of any area (or admin) |
| `authorizeRoles(...roles)` | Any listed role (or admin) |
| `canAccessArea(req.user, areaId)` | In controllers: admin, or area admin **of that area** |
| `isAdmin(user)`, `holdsAnyRole(user, roles)` | In controllers, when a rule depends on who is asking |

*   **Admins pass every role check.** Do not re-check for `"admin"` by hand; use the helpers.
*   Every route uses one of these guards. Only `/login`, `/dev-login`, and `/health` are public.
*   Guard the route in `*.routes.js` (`router.post("/", [authenticateAdmin], …)`); keep record-level checks in the controller.

## Status Codes for Access
| Situation | Status |
| --- | --- |
| No session, invalid or expired token | `401` |
| Signed in but missing the required role | `403` with the guard's role message |
| Record the user may not see (e.g. a non-admin reading another user) | `404` — do not reveal it exists |
| Self-lockout attempt (removing your own `admin` role, deleting yourself) | `400` with a message |

## Area Scoping
*   Area-scoped records (requests, vehicles, … in later features) are checked with `canAccessArea(req.user, areaId)` — never with inline role logic.
*   An area admin only reads and changes records of their own areas. Records outside the user's scope return `404`.
*   Centralize repeated scope queries in `app/authorization/` (e.g. a `where` builder for "areas this user administers") so controllers share one definition.

## Self-protection
*   An admin cannot remove their own `admin` role or delete their own account (`400`). The UI disables these too, but the API is the enforcement.
